+*   **When to Use**: Ideal for static or semi-static voxel terrain where mesh generation is not per-frame.
+*   **When Not to Use**: For dynamic voxel changes where per-frame culling is too slow, or when the underside of terrain must be visible.
 
 ## Background
-Face Culling in voxel rendering is a standard optimization technique in procedural terrain generation. The specific implementation details described here originate from a GitHub repository for voxel terrain generation [S1, unofficial]. The binary variant was documented in a devlog for a Unity-based voxel project [S2, unofficial].
+Face Culling is a fundamental optimization in voxel rendering, originating from the need to render large voxel worlds efficiently. It was popularized by games like Minecraft, where rendering every face of every cube would be computationally prohibitive. The specific implementation details described here, including the overhang check and bottom face culling, are documented in community devlogs and open-source projects [S1, unofficial]. The binary variant was developed to address performance bottlenecks in naive neighbor-checking implementations [S2, unofficial].
 
